WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.650
I'm a lazy language learner.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.590
I don't do tests.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.010
I don't do flashcards.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.360
I don't use the International
Phonetic Alphabet, the IPA.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:09.640
I don't go to class.

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.810
I study on my own when I feel like it.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.639
When I'm tired of whatever I'm
doing, I move on to something else.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.930
I fluctuate between difficult
content and easy content.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.670
The well known and outstanding Brazilian
educator described why comprehension tests

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.940
about things that we read are so futile
because reading is a vagabond experience.

00:00:30.999 --> 00:00:35.939
We don't want to challenge the reader
to try to remember or try to understand

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.619
or to explain what he or she read.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.150
We just want the reader
to enjoy the process.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.780
And so that's kind of been my approach.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.250
There are definitely arguments against
the sort of lazy language learning.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.330
Attitude.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.469
I've read a couple of books recently
that give reasons why we need to

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.349
be more deliberate, although with
the nuance, Outsmarting Your Brain

00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:00.910
by Dan Willingham, Make It Stick.

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:05.030
Now, these books do point out
that sort of deliberate block

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:09.259
learning, forcing yourself to learn
something is not very effective.

00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:15.300
However, they and others like Justin
Sung, whom you can find on YouTube,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.649
and I'll leave a link, and many
others point out that retrieval.

00:01:18.975 --> 00:01:22.725
Is very effective if you're forcing
yourself to try to remember something

00:01:22.755 --> 00:01:27.695
as in tests, the effort of retrieving
things helps anchor things in our long

00:01:27.695 --> 00:01:30.794
term memory, or you need to have it
structured so that you're learning

00:01:30.794 --> 00:01:36.054
things that are at that proximal
zone of capability or of knowledge so

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:39.244
that you're never attacking anything
that's too, too difficult for you.

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:41.464
And of course, that's the
difficulty in a classroom

00:01:41.464 --> 00:01:42.714
because the teacher doesn't know.

00:01:42.945 --> 00:01:48.524
What each student knows, so how can they
then give them just that next level,

00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:51.104
that proximal level of difficulty.

00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:55.114
And then there's Bloom's taxonomy where
it's not enough to retrieve things.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:02:00.694
We also have to develop the ability to
discuss, to evaluate, and to understand.

00:02:00.919 --> 00:02:04.600
Things that we are learning, but
that then brings me to why I think

00:02:04.699 --> 00:02:08.009
lazy language learning is the best
approach for language learning.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.600
When we're talking about language,
we're not talking about developing

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.570
or retaining with accuracy, bits of
information that we have learned.

00:02:16.649 --> 00:02:20.569
We're talking about using the content,
the material that's a hopefully of

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:22.310
interest to us to learn the language.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.270
When I read a history book on
Poland in Polish, I don't care

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.940
if I remember all the details.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:28.800
I'm not going to do it.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.565
Uh, a test on Polish history.

00:02:30.845 --> 00:02:37.385
I'm just aware that just by reading
and listening in Polish to whatever it

00:02:37.395 --> 00:02:43.505
might be, my ability in the language
is improving again, this zone of

00:02:43.505 --> 00:02:47.245
proximal knowledge, because we are
selecting things of interest to us.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.210
We are typically moving from one bit of
content in a particular area, using a

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:57.140
podcaster or an audio book or whatever
it might be, to something that is

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.070
more or less related to that subject.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:03.709
So we are helping ourselves, both in
terms of the subject matter and in

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.870
terms of the vocabulary, stay within
our area of proximal knowledge.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.900
Similarly, when I start out in a language.

00:03:11.205 --> 00:03:14.935
I do a lot of repetitive listening to
our mini stories because there, again,

00:03:14.955 --> 00:03:20.075
I'm staying within the limits of my
sort of proximal level of knowledge.

00:03:21.105 --> 00:03:25.605
Another advantage of this lazy approach
is that it keeps me motivated because

00:03:25.835 --> 00:03:30.415
whatever theoretically can be said
about the advantages of retrieval

00:03:30.415 --> 00:03:34.434
practice and testing and these
other things, if it demotivates me.

00:03:34.685 --> 00:03:36.575
Then it actually is counterproductive.

00:03:36.575 --> 00:03:42.115
Similarly with, say, trying to
learn the basics of grammar upfront.

00:03:42.115 --> 00:03:47.154
If I find that a demotivating thing to
do, even if it could be proven that it

00:03:47.154 --> 00:03:51.404
helps me master the grammar, which I don't
believe to be the case, if it demotivates

00:03:51.435 --> 00:03:52.925
me, then it's a bad thing to do.

00:03:54.855 --> 00:03:59.825
Given that retrieval is an important
part of developing long term memory,

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:03.035
so then some might argue we should
be doing tests to force us to

00:04:03.035 --> 00:04:04.675
retrieve things that we have learned.

00:04:04.735 --> 00:04:08.925
However, when we are listening and
reading, covering a lot of different

00:04:09.104 --> 00:04:13.494
types of material, maybe some of the same
essential subject matter using different

00:04:13.494 --> 00:04:18.245
books or different sources, we are in
fact forcing ourselves to retrieve things.

00:04:18.575 --> 00:04:23.155
That we have in our memory, so we don't
have to have a deliberate retrieval

00:04:23.344 --> 00:04:27.164
practice or exercise that's going to
force us to retrieve things by the mere

00:04:27.164 --> 00:04:31.854
fact that we are reading and listening
to things where there are words that

00:04:31.854 --> 00:04:35.174
are in various stages of being acquired.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.890
So in the case of LingQ, if
they're white, we know them.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.350
If they're different shades
of yellow, we're in different

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.530
stages of learning them.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.630
If they're blue, they're brand new.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.810
By listening and reading, we are again,
retrieving them so that the exercise

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:52.909
of retrieving takes place naturally
in an input based learning style.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.749
The big thing of course,
is to remain curious.

00:04:56.799 --> 00:04:58.920
It's our curiosity that drives us.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.470
Curiosity about the
language, about the people.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:06.450
So as long as we have that curiosity and
we have that sort of lazy attitude, it's

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:12.600
almost the sort of Wu Wei effortless
attitude of Zen or of Zhuangzi in

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:16.909
Chinese philosophy, we're going to be
moving forward, even though we have.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.950
A lazy approach to language learning,
and we resist the testing and the

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.040
other things that traditional classroom
instruction tries to force on us.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:31.139
And I think we are living in the golden
age of vagabond or lazy language learning.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.430
Never has there been a greater
variety of resources available through

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.260
YouTube, through podcasts, and I now
discovered that I can go to a podcast.

00:05:41.594 --> 00:05:47.205
And even if the MP3 file isn't available,
I can turn on the audio, open up

00:05:47.205 --> 00:05:52.695
Audacity, get an MP3 file, and then
I can bring that MP3 file into LingQ.

00:05:52.805 --> 00:05:54.825
LingQ will automatically transcribe it.

00:05:54.855 --> 00:05:55.694
I have a lesson.

00:05:55.845 --> 00:06:00.874
So the fields, the areas that we
can wander around, meander around

00:06:00.884 --> 00:06:04.535
like a hobo, like a vagabond,
as a lazy language learner.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.090
And increase our ability in the
language has never been greater.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.490
I have spoken previously on the
subject of lazy language learning,

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.430
and I will leave you a couple of
videos that I did at different stages.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:19.040
And with this video, I'm kind
of updating my position on this.

00:06:19.339 --> 00:06:20.270
Thank you for listening.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:20.959
Bye for now.

